# ── Caddyfile — HTTPS in front of Jarvis's dashboard/API ─────────────────────
# Why this exists: the new Loads feature's camera capture uses getUserMedia(),
# which browsers refuse to run outside a "secure context" (https://, or
# http://localhost). Jarvis is currently reached at a bare IP over plain
# http:// (port 8080, no TLS anywhere in the repo or deploy notes) — camera
# buttons will fail with a permission error until this is in front of it.
#
# Caddy auto-provisions and auto-renews a real Let's Encrypt certificate with
# zero manual cert management, as long as a domain name points at this VM.
# It replaces nothing in Jarvis itself — Jarvis keeps running on :8080
# exactly as it does today; Caddy just terminates TLS on :443 and forwards
# to it.
#
# ── REQUIRED before this works: you need a domain (or subdomain) with a DNS
# A record pointing at 35.233.131.198. Don't have one? Two options:
#   1. Cheapest: buy/use any domain you already control, add an A record
#      (e.g. jarvis.yourdomain.com -> 35.233.131.198), replace the line below.
#   2. No domain at all: skip Caddy, use a Cloudflare Tunnel instead — gives
#      you HTTPS on a *.trycloudflare.com URL with no DNS purchase. Ask if
#      you want that path instead; it's a different (also small) setup.
#
# Replace the placeholder domain below, then see the install steps in
# HTTPS_SETUP.md (also added to this repo) for the exact commands to run ON
# THE VM. I don't have SSH access to 35.233.131.198, so I can't run these for
# you — this file and the doc are everything needed once you SSH in.

# FILLED IN 2026-09-01. Apsara already owns edgemetals.com (it is the mail
# domain this whole system reads and sends from), so no purchase was ever
# needed — this file sat waiting on a domain she already had.
#
# A SUBDOMAIN, deliberately. One A record for jarvis.edgemetals.com changes
# nothing else about the zone. Do NOT move edgemetals.com's DNS to another
# provider to do this: the MX records for bose@ and apsara@ live there, and
# Jarvis reads that mailbox. Breaking email to fix a login would be a poor
# trade. Add the one record where the DNS already is.
# ── THE REPO COPY NOW MATCHES THE LIVE SERVER — 2026-10-05 ───────────────
# This file did NOT carry the HTTP/3 fix. It was applied on the VM on
# 2026-10-03 and never committed here, so a fresh clone plus the documented
# `sudo cp Caddyfile /etc/caddy/Caddyfile` would have switched HTTP/3 back on
# and reintroduced the outage. The server was right and the source of truth
# was wrong, which is the worst way round: the regression would arrive on a
# rebuild, months later, looking like a new bug.
#
# Copied from docs/cloudflare-runbook.md's "THE CURRENT CADDYFILE, AND WHY",
# which is the record of what is actually running.
#
# WHY protocols h1 h2. Caddy advertises HTTP/3 by default over UDP 443 with
#
#     alt-svc: h3=":443"; ma=2592000
#
# ma=2592000 is THIRTY DAYS and clients cache it. Many wifi and corporate
# networks pass TCP 443 and silently drop UDP 443, so those clients keep
# reaching for QUIC and failing while mobile data works. Six theories were
# checked on 2026-10-02/03 — expired certificate, blocked IP, bad resolver,
# TLS-intercepting proxy, IP reputation, key type — and this was the only
# survivor, because it is the only one that can behave differently per
# network.
#
# THE TRAP: turning it off here stops Caddy ADVERTISING it and does nothing
# about what a client already stored. A browser that cached the
# advertisement before 2026-10-03 stays broken for up to 30 days — until
# roughly 2026-11-02 — unless it is restarted or its cache cleared. "I
# changed it and it did not help" is not evidence the change was wrong.
#
# `servers` is a GLOBAL option. I first wrote it inside the site block,
# which Caddy rejects — that would have failed the config reload and taken
# the site down to fix a problem the site did not have.
#
# key_type rsa2048 is INERT until the certificate is reissued; Caddy keeps a
# valid one. Kept because the live file has it and this file must not drift
# from the live file again.
#
# Cloudflare was considered and DECLINED, on her reasoning and it is sound:
# in Full (strict) mode Cloudflare decrypts the traffic, so supplier payments
# and bank details would cross a third party in plaintext. That objection got
# STRONGER now that bank matching is in this repo.
{
	servers {
		protocols h1 h2
	}
}

jarvis.edgemetals.com {
	tls {
		key_type rsa2048
	}
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8080
}
